WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.022 --> 00:00:04.851
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.851 --> 00:00:10.640
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; A quick show of hands.

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:13.130
How many of you remember
playing with these as kids?

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:15.830
Awesome.

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:21.710
I for one can close my eyes and reminisce
the absolute sense of imagination and

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:23.680
creativity that these blocks could evoke.

00:00:24.885 --> 00:00:30.120
But imagine if you could use these
blocks to create a world of your own,

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:31.810
a real world.

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:36.140
So that could be something
like you being sent back in

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:40.680
time to 1624 to recreate
the city of New York.

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:43.870
How cool would that be?

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:45.889
Can you imagine the endless possibilities?

00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:49.700
Well, that's exactly
the feeling that I got

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:52.650
when I looked at my job
description about six years back.

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:54.990
The project in this instance,

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:59.290
was the first of its kind,
city within a city concept in the world.

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:02.230
It was called
the Dubai International Financial Center.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:04.180
So, as it happened,

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:08.090
the government of Dubai had latched
onto this opportunity that there was no

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:12.930
financial center which was serving
the region between London and Singapore.

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:17.120
Now, I probably know what some
of you are thinking here.

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:21.000
You're probably thinking well we know,
this region has a lot of potential.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:25.245
But then, we also know that this region
is synonymous with certain other words.

00:01:25.245 --> 00:01:32.080
Red tape, corruption, poor infrastructure,
social and political turmoil.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:33.611
And you would be absolutely correct.

00:01:37.024 --> 00:01:42.400
However, we are not today here
to talk about the present.

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:45.490
We are here to talk about a future,

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:49.740
a future in which you all
will be global leaders.

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:54.220
So how about we take a look at
the macroeconomic future for

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:54.880
this part of the world?

00:01:56.570 --> 00:02:01.700
If we were having this conversation in
say, 2009, this region's overall economic

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:06.080
output would have been something around
40% of the size of the U.S. economy.

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:09.780
Fast forward onto 2020.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:16.320
This number could be as high as 55%.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:20.580
And by 2030, this number could be 70% or
even higher.

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:24.300
In a sense,
this region would be imperative for

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:27.200
your business,
no matter what business you're in.

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:32.210
And the cool part is that the trends

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:34.477
which are changing these
numbers are already underway.

00:02:34.477 --> 00:02:39.470
About a decade back, if you were running
an international business, your business

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:44.430
would have been a part of what was known
as the East West Trading Corridor.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:48.070
And the East West Trade Corridor
used to contribute to

00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:52.230
about $8.5 out of every $10
exchanging hands globally.

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:56.270
However, in the last decade,

00:02:56.270 --> 00:03:00.200
there is another force which is changing
the landscape of global trade completely,

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:05.350
and this phenomenon, ladies and
gentlemen, is known as a new Silk Route.

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:10.712
Let's try to understand this phenomena
through the eyes of our first protagonist,

00:03:10.712 --> 00:03:12.147
Chun Lee.

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:16.460
Now, Chun Lee is a first
generation Chinese entrepreneur.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:22.660
She started a small disposal medical
supplies business back in 2003.

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:27.460
She was catering clients to predominantly
in Europe and in the Americas.

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:30.849
However, as we all know,
by 2009, things had changed.

00:03:30.849 --> 00:03:32.463
The world was different.

00:03:32.463 --> 00:03:36.943
Poor economic conditions and
the fact that regulations had become

00:03:36.943 --> 00:03:41.669
really stringent in the West,
forced her to consider other markets.

00:03:41.669 --> 00:03:45.146
It was at that point in time that she
heard about this whole story of growth

00:03:45.146 --> 00:03:47.323
in Africa, and India, and the Middle East.

00:03:47.323 --> 00:03:49.304
Not surprisingly, 16 months later,

00:03:49.304 --> 00:03:52.310
Chun Lee was on a plane
halfway across the globe.

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:56.410
And she started a very small re-export
hub in Dubai with just five employees.

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:02.530
In 2010, I was fortunate
enough to meet Chun Lee again.

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:06.200
And this time around boy,
how the fortune's changed.

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:11.610
Her five man operation had
grown to over 100 employees.

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:15.026
And her business had grown 1200%.

00:04:15.026 --> 00:04:18.740
And let me assure you,
Chun Lee is not an exception.

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:23.780
In fact, according to the World Bank, this
zone, or the South South Trading corridor,

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:28.870
or the New Silk Route,
is gonna contribute to $7 out of every $10

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:33.610
exchanging hands globally by 2030.

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:36.400
I saw the proof of this
growth first time myself,

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:39.800
from the city that I was
privileged to start.

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:44.400
When we started in 2004, these were
the clients which were registered with us.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:47.090
20 of them, and these are all of them.

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:51.760
I would love you to guess as to how
many clients did the city host today.

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:59.350
1400 plus and this is just a small
sample of the kind of clients it hosts.

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:06.340
But then, the business and economics
is just one part of the equation.

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:11.050
There is a far greater social force
which is completely transforming this

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:11.610
part of the world.

00:05:13.810 --> 00:05:15.720
Now let me ask you all a question
at this point in time.

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:20.420
If I ask all of you which country or
region in the world today

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:24.020
produces the highest proportion of women
entrepreneurs, what would your answer be?

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:28.330
I'll bet a lot of you would
be saying Silicon Valley,

00:05:28.330 --> 00:05:32.340
but you would be surprised if said no,
it is not Silicon Valley.

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:34.480
But in fact, it's in places like Yemen and

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:37.650
Uganda where 40% of
the entrepreneurs are women.

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:42.920
Let's try to understand this phenomena
through the eyes of our second

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:44.230
protagonist, Nigella.

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:48.820
Nigella is a mother of four and
she's a Saudi national.

00:05:50.090 --> 00:05:51.794
Ever since she was 16,

00:05:51.794 --> 00:05:56.437
she always wanted to start her own
private brand of clothing label.

00:05:56.437 --> 00:06:00.420
Unfortunately, the society did
not give her much of a chance.

00:06:01.890 --> 00:06:06.770
In fact, apart from a supportive husband,
nobody really came to help and added to

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:10.654
that, the regulations in Saudi Arabia for
women entrepreneurs was so stringent

00:06:10.654 --> 00:06:14.600
that she was not even allowed to represent
herself in front of the government.

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:20.350
And in spite of all these limitations,
Nigella finally broke through the glass

00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:24.675
ceiling and started her own private
label in 2012 and named it Sheila.

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:31.500
Now, having seen Nigella, and by the way,
Nigella is the one to your left.

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:35.140
Now, having seen Nigella and having
kinda heard her story, do you believe

00:06:35.140 --> 00:06:38.570
you really understand the needs and
aspirations of Nigella as a customer?

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:42.170
And this is where most
multinationals make a mistake.

00:06:43.170 --> 00:06:46.570
They pick up a product,
a service from one part of the world and

00:06:46.570 --> 00:06:49.999
take it to another part without really
understanding the ground realities.

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:54.060
Did you really understand the trials and
tribulations of Nigella?

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:58.410
Do you really understand
the environment that she operates in?

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:04.172
It won't really justify that
Nigella gives her business to you.

00:07:04.172 --> 00:07:07.420
So now, going back to our
example of the Lego bricks,

00:07:08.630 --> 00:07:12.520
this region is like a pack of Lego bricks
where all the pieces are coming together.

00:07:13.730 --> 00:07:18.580
Some of these pieces are broken,
some are not there and

00:07:18.580 --> 00:07:20.550
there are others which
are still under formation.

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:25.200
And it would be your responsibility as
future leaders to put all of these pieces

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:30.540
together and while you do that, you need
to remember that there is no precedence,

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:32.750
so you need to push the status quo.

00:07:32.750 --> 00:07:36.440
You need to be innovative, but
at the same time, you need to be patient,

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:37.650
and you need to be respectful.

00:07:37.650 --> 00:07:41.990
And there are a couple of principles
which I personally like to follow,

00:07:41.990 --> 00:07:44.210
which I would love to share with you all.

00:07:44.210 --> 00:07:48.880
First, read, but don't always take
the mass media too seriously.

00:07:50.110 --> 00:07:55.150
Secondly, learn, but start by unlearning.

00:07:55.150 --> 00:07:58.245
And finally,
every opportunity that you get go out and

00:07:58.245 --> 00:08:01.590
meet the Nigella and
the Chun Lee's of the world.

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:03.890
Try to soak in a little bit of
their culture and their life.

00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:07.742
And I must admit,
I made my own fair share of mistakes.

00:08:07.742 --> 00:08:11.650
In my first stint in the Middle East,
I was suppose to start

00:08:11.650 --> 00:08:14.449
a woman's only banking product for
one of the multinational clients.

00:08:15.490 --> 00:08:18.890
And I went into the market
imagining them to be my customers.

00:08:20.010 --> 00:08:22.170
And I was not totally off the mark.

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:25.760
They were in fact a part of the segment
that I was going to cater to.

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:27.320
But I made one fundamental mistake.

00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:29.153
I forgot the other end of the spectrum.

00:08:32.206 --> 00:08:36.444
So let me try to end this presentation
with one of my favorite quotes.

00:08:36.444 --> 00:08:42.167
The mind is like a parachute,
it does not work, if it doesn't open.

00:08:42.167 --> 00:08:43.410
Thank you.

00:08:43.410 --> 00:08:50.723
[APPLAUSE]

00:08:50.723 --> 00:08:55.750
[MUSIC]

